verdicts.reason:
Every kramdown measurement reproduces byte-exactly on kramdown 2.5.2 + kramdown-parser-gfm 1.1.0 — ```js → '<pre><code class="language-js">', ```mermaid → 'language-mermaid', ```js{1,3} → 'language-js{1,3}', ```js title="a.js" → '<p><code>js title="a.js"
const a=1;
</code></p>', and the default parser → '<p><code>js
const a=1;
</code></p>' with only ~~~js working. But the claim refutes a strawman on two axes. (1) It swaps 'unknown LANG' for 'info string containing whitespace'. `js title="a.js"` is a KNOWN lang plus a meta string. I measured the actual invariant: the unknown lang ```mdmax-meta survives as class="language-mdmax-meta" in 23 of 24 configs, INCLUDING kramdown with input: GFM. (2) 'FALSE for GitHub Pages' rests on kramdown's default parser, which GitHub Pages never uses. Primary read, jekyll/jekyll lib/jekyll/configuration.rb: line 39 `"markdown" => "kramdown"`, line 72 `"input" => "GFM"`. (3) The settled invariant in the project brief is about the compiler's own parse→serialize round-trip and about extensibility living in the VALUE of a field, not about third-party HTML rendering — the certificate cannot falsify a round-trip theorem with a renderer measurement.

